---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="ShouldISpend Methodology | How Spending Pressure Scores Work"
  description="Learn how ShouldISpend evaluates spending pressure using take-home income, savings, debt, outside help, emergency cushion, urgency, and financial flexibility."
>
  <main class="methodology-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>

      <h1>How ShouldISpend Calculates Spending Verdicts</h1>

      <p class="intro">
        ShouldISpend is built to help people slow down before major purchases,
        recurring commitments, and financial decisions that could create stress
        later. The calculators are educational pressure tests. They are not
        personal financial advice, but they are designed to show whether a
        decision looks comfortable, manageable, aggressive, or risky based on
        the numbers a user enters.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The Core Philosophy</h2>

      <p>
        Most spending decisions are not good or bad by themselves. A $5,000
        vacation, $700 car payment, $2,500 rent payment, $15,000 wedding, or
        $10,000 emergency expense can be reasonable for one household and
        dangerous for another. The difference is not only the price. It is the
        pressure that price creates.
      </p>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend focuses on the relationship between the cost and the
        user's broader financial situation. That usually means income, savings,
        debt, emergency reserves, monthly obligations, outside help, urgency,
        and the amount of breathing room left after the decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        The site is intentionally practical. A calculator verdict should not
        merely say whether someone can technically make a payment. It should
        help answer a better question: will this still feel manageable after the
        money is gone, the bill arrives, or the obligation becomes part of
        normal monthly life?
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What a Pressure Score Means</h2>

      <p>
        A ShouldISpend pressure score is a plain-language signal. Lower pressure
        generally means the decision appears affordable, flexible, and unlikely
        to weaken the user's financial position. Higher pressure means the
        decision may drain savings, increase debt stress, crowd out basic needs,
        or leave too little margin for surprises.
      </p>

      <p>
        The score is not meant to predict the future perfectly. It is meant to
        highlight tradeoffs. A high-pressure result does not always mean a user
        must say no. A low-pressure result does not mean a user should spend
        carelessly. The verdict is a starting point for judgment, not a command.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Take-Home Income Is the Default Income Rule</h2>

      <p>
        When a calculator asks for monthly income, ShouldISpend generally means
        take-home pay, not gross salary. Take-home pay is the money actually
        available after taxes, payroll deductions, and other withheld amounts.
      </p>

      <p>
        This matters because spending pressure is felt against real cash flow.
        A household that earns a high gross salary may still feel pressure if
        taxes, insurance, retirement contributions, debt payments, childcare,
        rent, or other obligations consume much of that income before spending
        begins.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some calculators use annual income when that better fits the decision,
        but the same principle applies: the site tries to measure affordability
        against usable financial capacity, not an inflated number that ignores
        what actually reaches the household.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How Outside Help, Aid, Insurance, and Gifts Are Treated</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend calculators should not punish users for costs they do not
        personally have to cover. If family help, scholarships, grants,
        employer reimbursement, insurance, savings already set aside, or another
        reliable source fully covers a cost, pressure can fall sharply.
      </p>

      <p>
        In some cases, outside help can reduce the user's pressure score to
        zero. For example, if a college expense, medical bill, wedding cost, or
        repair is fully covered without borrowing and without draining emergency
        savings, the user's personal financial pressure may be minimal.
      </p>

      <p>
        The key question is whether the uncovered cost still creates strain. A
        large sticker price does not automatically mean high pressure if the
        user's actual out-of-pocket responsibility is small or fully covered.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>No Artificial Minimum Pressure</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend does not use artificial minimum pressure just because a
        purchase is large, emotional, optional, or expensive in general. If the
        numbers show that a user can comfortably afford the decision, preserve
        emergency savings, avoid borrowing, and still maintain flexibility, the
        score should be allowed to be low.
      </p>

      <p>
        That also means pressure can be zero in clear affordability cases. Huge
        savings, very high take-home income, full outside help, full insurance
        coverage, or a tiny uncovered cost with no borrowing can all justify a
        zero-pressure result.
      </p>

      <p>
        Soft preference penalties should not override obvious affordability. A
        calculator may still explain that a decision is optional, emotional, or
        worth comparing against alternatives, but those comments should not
        force pressure when the financial burden is clearly manageable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why Urgent Needs Are Treated Differently</h2>

      <p>
        Not every spending decision is optional. Medical bills, essential home
        repairs, funeral expenses, transportation needs, and safety-related
        costs can be necessary even when they create pressure.
      </p>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend tries to separate affordability from necessity. A needed
        expense can still be high-pressure, but the guidance should focus on
        reducing damage, avoiding the worst financing choices, preserving basic
        stability, and finding safer payment options when possible.
      </p>

      <p>
        Optional upgrades are treated differently. A luxury vacation, larger
        wedding, expensive car payment, or lifestyle upgrade may deserve more
        caution if it weakens emergency savings or creates debt. The site does
        not treat all spending as equal because the stakes are not equal.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Common Factors the Calculators Consider</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Income:</strong> whether the cost fits within usable household
          income or monthly take-home pay.
        </li>

        <li>
          <strong>Savings:</strong> whether the decision would drain cash,
          planned savings, or emergency reserves.
        </li>

        <li>
          <strong>Debt:</strong> whether existing obligations already create
          pressure before the new expense.
        </li>

        <li>
          <strong>Emergency cushion:</strong> whether the household could still
          handle a surprise bill after spending.
        </li>

        <li>
          <strong>Monthly obligations:</strong> whether rent, car payments,
          insurance, utilities, minimum debt payments, and other bills leave
          room for normal life.
        </li>

        <li>
          <strong>Borrowing risk:</strong> whether the decision depends on
          credit cards, high-interest debt, long repayment terms, or payment
          plans that may become stressful.
        </li>

        <li>
          <strong>Flexibility:</strong> whether the user still has room for
          savings, debt payoff, basic needs, and future goals afterward.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why Different Calculators Use Different Inputs</h2>

      <p>
        A rent calculator should not behave exactly like a wedding calculator.
        A grocery budget tool should not think the same way as a car payment
        calculator. A funeral payment calculator should not treat urgency like
        a vacation calculator does.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each page is built around the decision being evaluated. Some decisions
        are mainly about monthly cash flow. Others are about total cost,
        savings depletion, debt risk, or recovery time. The broad framework
        stays consistent, but the inputs and explanations are tailored to the
        specific financial decision.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Limits of the Calculators</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend calculators rely on simplified rules and the information a
        user enters. They cannot know every detail of a household's financial
        life, including taxes, local costs, credit score, insurance coverage,
        job security, health needs, family obligations, future income, or
        personal priorities.
      </p>

      <p>
        Results should be treated as educational estimates. A decision that
        looks risky may still be necessary. A decision that looks affordable may
        still be wrong for personal reasons. A calculator can provide context,
        but it cannot replace full personal judgment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Educational Use Only</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend does not provide personalized financial, legal, tax,
        insurance, medical, or investment advice. The site is not a lender,
        financial planning firm, insurance agency, debt settlement company, or
        investment adviser.
      </p>

      <p>
        Users should consider their full situation and consult qualified
        professionals when appropriate, especially for decisions involving large
        debts, legal obligations, taxes, insurance claims, medical bills, or
        long-term financial commitments.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can also read the <a href="/about">About</a> page,
        <a href="/disclaimer">Disclaimer</a>, <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a>,
        or contact the site through the <a href="/contact">Contact</a> page.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <style>
    .methodology-page {
      max-width: 900px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 72px 24px;
    }

    .hero {
      margin-bottom: 54px;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: #0f766e;
      font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
      line-height: 1;
      margin: 0 0 24px;
      color: #061126;
    }

    h2 {
      margin-top: 48px;
      margin-bottom: 14px;
      color: #061126;
      font-size: 1.9rem;
      line-height: 1.2;
    }

    p,
    li {
      color: #475569;
      line-height: 1.8;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
    }

    p {
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    li {
      margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    ul {
      padding-left: 1.4rem;
    }

    a {
      color: #0f766e;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .intro {
      font-size: 1.16rem;
      color: #334155;
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
